    {
      "id": "1a1e8ecf68c0-q4",
      "number": 4,
      "question": "If x 2 + y 2 = 6 and x 3 y + xy 3 + x 2 + y 2 = 36, then the value of xy is :",
      "questionHtml": "If x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 6 and x<sup>3</sup>y + xy<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 36, then the value of xy is :",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "4",
          "html": "4"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "3",
          "html": "3"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "5",
          "html": "5"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "6",
          "html": "6"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "C",
      "solution": "<span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png' width='26px' /> <u style='font-size: 21px; font-weight: 700; display: inline !important;'>Shortcut Trick</u></span>\n<p>Factor out the <strong>common factor</strong> (x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) from the given expression: xy(x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) + (x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) = 36</p>\n<p>Substitute the value x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 6 into the equation: 6(xy) + 6 = 36</p>\n<p>⇒ 6(xy + 1) = 36</p>\n<p>⇒ xy + 1 = 6 ⇒ xy = 5</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 5.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png' width='26px' /><strong><span style='vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;'><u>Alternate Method</u></span></strong></span></p>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n<p>x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 6 --- (1)</p>\n<p>x<sup>3</sup>y + xy<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 36 --- (2)</p>\n<p><strong>Method: Factorization by grouping</strong></p>\n<p>⇒ xy(x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) + (x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) = 36</p>\n<p>⇒ (x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>)(xy + 1) = 36</p>\n<p><strong>Substitute</strong> the value of (x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>) from equation (1):</p>\n<p>⇒ 6 × (xy + 1) = 36</p>\n<p>⇒ xy + 1 = 36 ÷ 6</p>\n<p>⇒ xy + 1 = 6</p>\n<p>⇒ xy = 6 − 1</p>\n<p>⇒ xy = 5</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 5.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png' width='26px' /><strong><span style='vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;'><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Factorization by Grouping</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>This method involves rearranging the terms of a <strong>polynomial</strong> into groups that share a <strong>common factor</strong>, such as a(x + y) + b(x + y) = (a + b)(x + y).</p>\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Algebraic Identity for Squares</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>The square of a <strong>binomial</strong> is given by (x + y)<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> + 2xy. This is useful for finding the sum of squares when the product is known.</p>\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Substitution Method</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>In a <strong>system of equations</strong>, a known value or expression from one equation is substituted into another to reduce the number of <strong>variables</strong> and solve the problem.</p>",
      "images": [
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png"
      ],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1a1e8ecf68c0-q5",
      "number": 5,
      "question": "Simplify- \\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-8(0.39)^3]}=?\\)",
      "questionHtml": "<p>Simplify-</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-8(0.39)^3]}=?\\)</span></p>\n",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "-3",
          "html": "-3"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "1",
          "html": "1"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "-1",
          "html": "-1"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "3",
          "html": "3"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "C",
      "solution": "<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-8(0.39)^3]}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p><strong>Concept used:</strong></p>\n\n<p>If a + b + c = 0, then a<sup>3</sup> + b<sup>3</sup> + c<sup>3</sup> = 3abc</p>\n\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-8(0.39)^3]}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p>&rArr;&nbsp;<span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-2^3(0.39)^3]}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{[(0.37)^3+(0.41)^3-(0.78)^3]}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p>Now,</p>\n\n<p>0.37 + 0.41 &ndash; 0.78 = 0,</p>\n\n<p>then 0.37<sup>3</sup> + 0.41<sup>3</sup> &ndash; 0.78<sup>3</sup> = &ndash; 3 &times; 0.37 &times; 0.41 &times; 0.78</p>\n\n<p>So,</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times0.74\\times1.23\\times0.13}{– 3 × 0.37 × 0.41 × 0.78}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3\\times2\\times0.37\\times3\\times0.41\\times0.13}{– 3 × 0.37 × 0.41 × 0.78}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span><span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\frac{3 × 0.37 × 0.41 × 0.78}{– 3 × 0.37 × 0.41 × 0.78}\\)</span></p>\n\n<p>&rArr; - 1</p>\n\n<p><strong>&there4; The required answer is - 1.</strong></p>\n",
